  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We will employ a multi-resolution optimization strategy to escape local optima and find a global minimum for the Erds minimum overlap problem. The algorithm will:\n   - Start with a population-based optimization (Differential Evolution) on a coarse grid to find the best global shape\n   - Use dimensionality reduction to monitor progress and detect plateaus\n   - Gradually increase resolution to 400 points to improve solution quality\n   - Apply local refinement using gradient-based optimization to polish the solution\n   - Monitor improvement trends and automatically adapt hyperparameters to fit remaining time\n   This differs from the previous work by combining global search with adaptive resolution changes and using a fallback mechanism when improvement stalls.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Variables:\n   - h: piecewise constant function with n_points samples\n   - C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over x\u2208[0,2]\n   Optimization variables:\n   - h values \u2208 [0,1] for each sample point\n   - n_points \u2208 {400, 800} possible grid sizes\n   Constraints:\n   - \u2211h_i \u0394x = 1, where \u0394x = 2/n_points\n   - h_i \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n   Objective:\n   - minimize C\u2085\n   Formulation:\n   The problem is implicitly defined through the provided compute_c5() function. We treat it as a non-convex optimization problem requiring:\n   - Custom gradient estimation for the unusual correlation-based objective\n   - Individual projection to maintain integral constraints during optimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Step 1 (Population initialization): Generate 100 feasible points with random noise and projection (seed=42)\n   - Step 2 (Coarse optimization): Run DE on 400-point grid with:\n        Max evaluations: int(min(0.4*budget, 15000))\n        Mutation factor: 0.9 to 0.95 dynamically\n        Step size: 0.1 * (budget/1300)**0.5\n   - Step 3 (Resolution detection): Check improvement rate over 100 evaluations\n        Compute improvement = best_c5 - current_c5\n        If improvement <= 2e-8 * best_c5, reduce resolution or population\n   - Step 4 (Refinement): \n        For 20 points in the best/border population:\n            Run SLSQP with p-norm regularization (p=1.5)\n            With adaptive box bounds: [max(0, h_i-0.05), min(1, h_i+0.05)]\n   - Step 5 (Fallback path): If no improvement for 100 evaluations:\n        Reduce grid size to 400 points with interpolation\n        Apply one final SGD step with momentum\n   - Constraint handling:\n        Use projection_feasible() after every evaluation\n        Incorporate dynamic barrier parameter \u03b1 in objective function\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Start with gridsize=800\n   - Primary: DE with adaptive popsize\n   - Secondary: SLSQP refinement\n   - Variations:\n        1. Dimensionality reduction: convert problem to self-similar grid\n        2. Stochastic initialization: jittered grid with random angle\n        3. Dual-targeted correlations: balance speed vs accuracy\n\n5. Compute budget:\n   - Coarse optimization: 20% wall time (estimate)\n   - Resolution expansion: 25% wall time\n   - Fine optimization: 30% wall time\n   - Local refinement: 25% wall time\n   Decision points:\n        if improvement_rate < 1e-5:\n            grid_size *= 0.8 (minimum 40)\n        else:\n            grid_size *= 1.25 (maximum 1600)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Validate h_values by: \n        0 <= min(h_values) <= max(h_values) <= 1\n        abs(sum(h_values)*dx - 1) <= 1e-6\n   - Compute C\u2085 independently using:\n        corr = np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode='full')\n        C\u2085 = max(corr) * (2.0/len(h_values))\n   - Fallback mechanism:\n        Maintain current best solution through derivative:\n            h[] = best_h + \u03b7 * (target_h - best_h)\n        where \u03b7 = 0.05 * (1 - time/time_limit)\n   - Termination:\n        if time > budget or improvement < 1e-8:\n            return current_best, compute_c5(current_best), len(current_best)",
